// What a 'mod' declaration refuses: the shape of it the grammar admits and the
// compiler does not build, and the in-file block that does not exist.
//
// 'mod name;' names the module a source file belongs to, and it claims the whole
// file: it is the file's first statement, and a file declares one module. A 'mod'
// anywhere else is ErrorModDcl, whether it is a second one or merely late.
//
// A 'mod name { ... }' block is no construct at all: a module is never declared
// inside a file, and a nested module is a file of its own or a subfolder with its
// own designated file.
// The parser recognises the block only to say so, and skips its body. 'mod trait'
// is admitted so that its spelling is settled rather than designed later, and is
// reported where it is written rather than accepted with nothing under it.
//
// Parse stage, which is why this is its own file: a parse diagnostic stops the
// compile before name resolution, so nothing here resolves and nothing else could
// be asserted alongside. Each declaration is at the top level, where recovery from
// one reaches the next -- a refused form's body is skipped whole rather than read
// on as global statements.
//
// The accepted header is first, so that everything after it is refused for its own
// reason and not for following a bad one.

mod modparse;

mod inner {                  //~ ErrorUnbuiltKind "A module cannot be declared inside a file: a nested module is a file of its own, or a subfolder with its own designated file"
  fn helper() i64 {
    1i64;
  }
}

mod trait Served;            //~ ErrorUnbuiltKind "names a module's abstraction"

mod again;                   //~ ErrorModDcl "first statement"

// 'pub' on a module declaration says the module is visible outside its PARENT,
// and only a submodule has one: a subfolder holding its own designated file is
// private to the module whose folder holds it unless its own declaration says
// otherwise. A module that is a file of its own is inside nothing, so there is
// nothing for 'pub' to open it to. The placement is refused as well, because the
// two are separate facts about the same declaration: this file's module is named
// already, and would be whether or not 'pub' were written
pub mod outer;               //~ ErrorBadPub "has no parent"
                             //~^ ErrorModDcl "first statement"

mod;                         //~ ErrorNoName

// A declaration after the last refusal, so that recovery is shown to land back on
// the global statement stream rather than swallowing the rest of the file.
fn tail() i64 {
  2i64;
}
